{
  "technical_and_design_decisions": [
    "Target platform is the Spring Cloud Data Flow (SCDF) acceptance test suite, since the referenced modules (null, rich_gauge, router, shell, splunk, throughput-sampler sinks and spark-app, timestampfile batch jobs) are SCDF stream sink and batch task applications.",
    "Tests will be written in Java 17 (required by Spring Framework 6.0) using JUnit 5 and Spring Boot Test, following the existing acceptance test module conventions.",
    "Each user story is implemented as one acceptance test class that deploys a stream (for sinks) or launches a task (for batch jobs) against a running local SCDF test environment (docker-compose based) and asserts observable end-to-end behavior.",
    "Shared test harness utilities will be created/extended for: stream definition and deployment, task launch, log/file assertion, and polling-with-timeout verification helpers, so per-module tests stay small and consistent.",
    "Splunk acceptance test will use a Splunk Docker container in the test environment and verify events by querying the Splunk REST search API after data is sent through the stream.",
    "Spark-app batch acceptance test will run against a local/embedded Spark (local[*] master) environment provisioned in the test setup rather than an external cluster.",
    "Shell sink test verifies behavior by asserting on output files produced by the shell command in a mounted volume; timestampfile test asserts a file with an embedded timestamp is created in a known directory.",
    "Throughput-sampler test asserts statistical sampling behavior using a fixed message count and an acceptable tolerance band on the sampled count to avoid flakiness.",
    "Router sink test uses a routing expression with multiple named destinations and asserts that messages arrive only at the expected destination queues/topics.",
    "All tests are wired into the existing CI build so they run automatically on every build, with tests marked as acceptance-tier and executed against the dockerized environment."
  ],
  "assumptions": [
    "An existing acceptance test infrastructure and CI pipeline already exist and are being extended, as implied by the wording 'include acceptance test coverage'; no greenfield framework build is required.",
    "A local SCDF test environment (e.g., docker-compose) is available and can host the required sink apps, message broker (Kafka or RabbitMQ), Splunk container, and a local Spark runtime.",
    "Docker images or jars for all eight modules under test already exist and are deployable; only test code, fixtures, and configuration need to be developed.",
    "Estimates are for a single developer performing all work (analysis, implementation, debugging, CI integration, documentation, and rework) and represent effort in work-hours, not elapsed calendar time.",
    "One acceptance test class per user story with a small number of representative happy-path test cases per module is sufficient coverage for the stated QA goal.",
    "External system complexity (Splunk indexing latency, Spark job startup) is handled with polling/retry helpers and is accounted for in the per-activity estimates.",
    "No production code changes to the sink/batch modules themselves are required; any defects discovered during testing are out of scope for this estimate.",
    "Code review feedback and reasonable rework are included as a dedicated activity."
  ],
  "activities": [
    {
      "activity_id": "A1",
      "activity_name": "Requirements analysis and acceptance test design",
      "activity_description": "Analyze the eight user stories, inspect the existing acceptance test suite conventions, define the test approach per module (streams vs. tasks), identify required test environments (broker, Splunk, Spark, file mounts), and produce the test design/checklist for all modules.",
      "most_likely_effort": 8
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A2",
      "activity_name": "Shared test harness and fixtures setup",
      "activity_description": "Create/extend shared acceptance test utilities: stream definition/deployment helpers, task launch helpers, polling-with-timeout assertion utilities, file/log assertion helpers, Splunk REST search client wrapper, and docker-compose environment additions (Splunk container, Spark local mode, shared volumes).",
      "most_likely_effort": 16
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A3",
      "activity_name": "U1: null sink acceptance test",
      "activity_description": "Implement acceptance test that deploys a stream with the null sink, sends test messages, and verifies the stream deploys and runs successfully while messages are discarded without errors (verified via app logs and stream status).",
      "most_likely_effort": 4
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A4",
      "activity_name": "U2: rich_gauge sink acceptance test",
      "activity_description": "Implement acceptance test that deploys a stream with the rich-gauge sink, sends a known set of numeric values, and verifies the computed gauge (value, average, min, max, count) through the configured metrics/analytics store.",
      "most_likely_effort": 8
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A5",
      "activity_name": "U3: router sink acceptance test",
      "activity_description": "Implement acceptance test that deploys a stream with the router sink configured with a routing expression and multiple destinations, sends messages matching each route, and verifies each message arrives only at its expected destination.",
      "most_likely_effort": 8
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A6",
      "activity_name": "U4: shell sink acceptance test",
      "activity_description": "Implement acceptance test that deploys a stream with the shell sink executing a shell command on incoming payloads, sends test data, and verifies the expected output (e.g., files written to a mounted volume) is produced correctly.",
      "most_likely_effort": 6
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A7",
      "activity_name": "U5: splunk sink acceptance test",
      "activity_description": "Implement acceptance test that deploys a stream with the splunk sink targeting the containerized Splunk instance, sends identifiable test events, and verifies the events are indexed in Splunk by querying the Splunk REST search API with polling until indexed.",
      "most_likely_effort": 12
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A8",
      "activity_name": "U6: throughput-sampler sink acceptance test",
      "activity_description": "Implement acceptance test that deploys a stream with the throughput-sampler sink at a configured sample rate, sends a fixed number of messages, and verifies the sampled throughput count falls within an acceptable tolerance band of the expected rate.",
      "most_likely_effort": 8
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A9",
      "activity_name": "U7: spark-app batch job acceptance test",
      "activity_description": "Implement acceptance test that launches the spark-app batch job as a task against the local Spark runtime, provides input data, waits for job completion, and verifies the job exit status and expected output/results.",
      "most_likely_effort": 14
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A10",
      "activity_name": "U8: timestampfile batch job acceptance test",
      "activity_description": "Implement acceptance test that launches the timestampfile batch job as a task and verifies that a file with an embedded timestamp in its name is created in the expected directory with the expected content.",
      "most_likely_effort": 6
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A11",
      "activity_name": "CI pipeline integration",
      "activity_description": "Wire all new acceptance tests into the CI build so they execute automatically on every build (build configuration, test environment provisioning step, test reporting), and verify a full green CI run.",
      "most_likely_effort": 6
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A12",
      "activity_name": "Test stabilization and flakiness remediation",
      "activity_description": "Run the new tests repeatedly in the CI environment, diagnose timing/environment-related failures (Splunk indexing delays, Spark startup, broker readiness), and harden tests with retries, polling, and proper cleanup.",
      "most_likely_effort": 8
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A13",
      "activity_name": "Documentation",
      "activity_description": "Document the new acceptance tests: module coverage list, how to run the tests locally and in CI, environment prerequisites (Splunk container, Spark local mode), and troubleshooting notes.",
      "most_likely_effort": 3
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A14",
      "activity_name": "Code review and rework",
      "activity_description": "Prepare changes for review, address reviewer feedback on test code, harness utilities, and CI configuration, and re-verify affected tests after rework.",
      "most_likely_effort": 5
    }
  ],
  "reported_total": {
    "most_likely_effort": 112
  }
}
```

